doc:Factsarticle1_141 schema1:abstract "Currently, thermal power units undertake the task of peak and frequency regulation, and their internal equipment is in a non-conventional environment, which could very easily fail and thus lead to unplanned shutdown of the unit. To realize the condition monitoring and early warning of the key equipment inside coal power units, this study proposes a deep learning-based equipment condition anomaly detection model, which combines the deep autoencoder (DAE), Transformer, and Gaussian mixture model (GMM) to establish an anomaly detection model. DAE and the Transformer encoder extract static and time-series features from multi-dimensional operation data, and GMM learns the feature distribution of normal data to realize anomaly detection. Based on the data verification of boiler superheater equipment and turbine bearings in real power plants, the model is more capable of detecting equipment anomalies in advance than the traditional method and is more stable with fewer false alarms. When applied to the superheater equipment, the proposed model triggered early warnings approximately 90 h in advance compared to the actual failure time, with a lower false negative rate, reducing the missed detection rate by 70% compared to the Transformer-GMM (TGMM) model, which verifies the validity of the model and its early warning capability." .
_:a64f9c3d9f1da0f6da1446c466b144a1 rdf:reifies <<( doc:Factsarticle1_141 schema1:abstract "Currently, thermal power units undertake the task of peak and frequency regulation, and their internal equipment is in a non-conventional environment, which could very easily fail and thus lead to unplanned shutdown of the unit. To realize the condition monitoring and early warning of the key equipment inside coal power units, this study proposes a deep learning-based equipment condition anomaly detection model, which combines the deep autoencoder (DAE), Transformer, and Gaussian mixture model (GMM) to establish an anomaly detection model. DAE and the Transformer encoder extract static and time-series features from multi-dimensional operation data, and GMM learns the feature distribution of normal data to realize anomaly detection. Based on the data verification of boiler superheater equipment and turbine bearings in real power plants, the model is more capable of detecting equipment anomalies in advance than the traditional method and is more stable with fewer false alarms. When applied to the superheater equipment, the proposed model triggered early warnings approximately 90 h in advance compared to the actual failure time, with a lower false negative rate, reducing the missed detection rate by 70% compared to the Transformer-GMM (TGMM) model, which verifies the validity of the model and its early warning capability." )>> ;
